 * (1) JSON nested so deeply that the parser gives up is a 400 `bad_envelope` (counted in `rejected_envelopes`), not a 500; for `/resolve` it is a 400 `bad_decision`.
 * (2) The stored copy of the payload escapes every non-ASCII character (`ensure_ascii`), so a JSON escape for half a character (a lone surrogate) is stored as text; a name, email, phone, company or message that contains one cannot be stored and gives verdict `review` with reason `bad_field:<field>`.
 * (3) An email whose lower-case form is longer than 254 characters (some letters become two when lower-cased) gives verdict `review`, reason `invalid_email`, not a 500.
-* (3b) An email whose domain (the part after the @) is badly shaped gives verdict `review`, reason `invalid_email`: no lead of its own, no follow-up mail. Badly shaped means: no dot in the domain, a label (a part between dots) with no characters (a domain that starts with a dot, ends with a dot or has two dots in a row), or a label that starts or ends with a hyphen. The domain needs a real dot (the ordinary full stop of the keyboard); U+3002, U+FF0E and U+FF61 only split a label, so a domain whose only dots are those three is refused. A label that starts or ends with a look-alike of the hyphen (U+FF0D, U+2010, U+2011, U+2212), and a domain that holds a look-alike of the dot (U+2024, U+FE52), are refused too; nothing is folded to another form, so a valid internationalised name (`xn--k.test`, `bücher.test`) stays accepted. The sender, recipient and reminder addresses must also hold exactly one @. The same label rule refuses the sender, recipient and reminder addresses (`DESK_SMTP_FROM`, `DESK_SMTPP_FROM`, `DESK_SMTPP_RECIPIENTS`, `DESK_NUDGE_TO`). Not checked: a label longer than 63 characters, dots or hyphens in the part before the @, and an IP address in square brackets after the @. The tests are in `tests/test_domain_labels.py`.
+* (3b) An email whose domain (the part after the @) is badly shaped gives verdict `review`, reason `invalid_email`: no lead of its own, no follow-up mail. Badly shaped means: no dot in the domain, a label (a part between dots) with no characters (a domain that starts with a dot, ends with a dot or has two dots in a row), or a label that starts or ends with a hyphen. The domain needs a real dot (the ordinary full stop of the keyboard); U+3002, U+FF0E and U+FF61 only split a label, so a domain whose only dots are those three is refused. A label that starts or ends with a look-alike of the hyphen (U+FF0D, U+2010, U+2011, U+2212), and a domain that holds a look-alike of the dot (U+2024, U+FE52), are refused too; nothing is folded to another form, so a valid internationalised name (`xn--k.test`, `bücher.test`) stays accepted. The sender, recipient and reminder addresses must also hold exactly one @. The dot rule is for the lead email only: a one-label host such as `localhost` is accepted for the sender, recipient and reminder addresses (`desk@localhost`). The same label rule refuses the sender, recipient and reminder addresses (`DESK_SMTP_FROM`, `DESK_SMTPP_FROM`, `DESK_SMTPP_RECIPIENTS`, `DESK_NUDGE_TO`). Not checked: a label longer than 63 characters, dots or hyphens in the part before the @, and an IP address in square brackets after the @. The tests are in `tests/test_domain_labels.py`.
 * (4) Only the thread that won the claim asks the AI step, the CRM or the sender, so two requests that drive the same new lead at the same moment, or a request and the reaper, ask the AI step once (the claim comes first; the reaper only drives `pending` rows, and a claimed row is `sending`); a CRM row can be put again only after a leftover intent mark, and then only on a keyed CRM, see the CRM row above.
   * A failed AI step after the claim is one automatic try (`internal:<class>`; the row goes back to `pending` with a backoff through `_abort_unsent`, and to `failed` only when the cap is already reached; after a person's Retry the count starts at 1, so the row is `pending`).
   * A lock on any write after the claim, the first stage stamp included, gives the try back instead (see the line on locks below), with one exception: a lock that stops the write of a model answer leaves the try used up (`ModelAnswerLost`, below).
